    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/01/adidas-adizero-evo-sl-review-2024.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The full-length Lightstrike Pro midsole is soft and comfortable with solid impact protection for training and longer efforts; heel landings feel smooth from the posterolateral bevel, and the forefoot rolls easily with a mildly flexible feel.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is moderate at first but improves after break-in (around 20 miles), with more bounce at faster paces; the nylon shank/plate adds stiffness and noticeable snappiness on pickups.",
        "Stability: This is a neutral shoe with a narrower midfoot, but shank-driven torsional rigidity helps transitions; the heel geometry adds slight guidance, while the heel counter is small and somewhat flexible rather than highly rigid.",
        "Durability: Midsole longevity appears good and improves with use, but outsole durability is a concern because posterolateral heel rubber began tearing around 45 miles; traction is strong, though exposed foam is vulnerable on rough surfaces."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/12/review-adidas-adizero-evo-sl-lot-of.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The full-length Lightstrike Pro midsole with a high 38.5/32 mm stack is repeatedly described as excellent for softness and impact protection, but heel-strike running at slower paces is noted as less favorable than forefoot/midfoot use.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is a standout, with testers highlighting strong bounce/propulsion from Lightstrike Pro and a 60% forefoot rocker that feels most effective at faster paces.",
        "Stability: A padded, fairly stiff internal heel counter provides secure heel hold and no major heel slip, but the thin roomy upper and non-gusseted tongue reduce lock-down and lateral security, especially on uneven terrain, corners, or cobblestones.",
        "Durability: The provided text does not report explicit outsole wear or long-term material longevity results; the upper is described as thin and cost-cut, which may imply weaker long-term robustness versus more structured trainers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/adidas-adizero-evo-sl-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The full Lightstrike Pro setup (38 mm heel / 32 mm forefoot) is described as soft, squishy, and smooth, with strong impact comfort for both short and long runs.",
        "Responsiveness: Ride is consistently called bouncy and responsive with buttery rebound, and the low weight helps pace pickups, though it is not as snappy as plated race shoes.",
        "Stability: Upper/heel hold is decent with a more structured heel counter, but the platform has limited guidance and support, so torsional/lateral stability is modest for runners needing control.",
        "Durability: Continental rubber outsole and overall construction are highlighted as quality; with 50+ miles logged, no notable outsole or material wear issues were reported."
      ]
    }
